WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.420 --> 00:00:03.960
Were you chanting "Hallway fight! Hallway fight!"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.960 --> 00:00:06.720
while watching the newest episode&nbsp;
of "She-Hulk: Attorney at Law?"

00:00:06.720 --> 00:00:12.360
The penultimate episode of "She-Hulk: Attorney at&nbsp;
Law" finally delivered the dose of Daredevil many&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.360 --> 00:00:18.600
Marvel Cinematic Universe fans have long been&nbsp;
hungering for. Charlie Cox finally returns as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.600 --> 00:00:23.820
the devil of Hell's Kitchen, who leaves the gritty&nbsp;
streets of Manhattan behind to defend a new client&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.820 --> 00:00:29.100
on the sunny shores of California. It's the first&nbsp;
opportunity fans get to spend some actual time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.100 --> 00:00:34.380
with the character following his extremely&nbsp;
brief cameo in "Spider-Man: No Way Home."

00:00:34.380 --> 00:00:40.440
Cox slips easily back into the role of Matthew&nbsp;
Murdock, lawyer by day and crime-buster by night.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.440 --> 00:00:45.060
Though he features a new costume design with gold&nbsp;
accents and seems to have loosened up a little,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.060 --> 00:00:49.920
personality-wise, this is unmistakably&nbsp;
the Daredevil fans fell in love with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.920 --> 00:00:54.374
back when he first appeared in his own&nbsp;
Netflix show, down to the theme song.

00:00:54.374 --> 00:00:55.920
"I'm Daredevil!"

00:00:55.920 --> 00:01:00.420
And it's a good thing, too. Netflix's&nbsp;
"Daredevil" set itself apart from the get-go;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:00.420 --> 00:01:05.220
it wasn't going to be like other superhero&nbsp;
media where physical damage is immaterial,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:05.220 --> 00:01:09.960
where our hero can fall from a roof or take&nbsp;
a roundhouse kick to the jaw without breaking&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.960 --> 00:01:15.420
a sweat. Instead, "Daredevil" allowed its&nbsp;
protagonist to take damage, making Matt feel&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.420 --> 00:01:20.580
more vulnerable and human. Another pleasant&nbsp;
surprise resulting from that choice is that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.580 --> 00:01:26.280
the fight scenes felt more intense. Sustaining&nbsp;
injuries meant that Matt often struggled visibly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.280 --> 00:01:31.620
toward the end of a fight, limping and straining&nbsp;
to keep himself upright as he battled his foes.

00:01:31.620 --> 00:01:34.140
Now, with his Disney+ debut,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:34.140 --> 00:01:38.880
"She-Hulk" paid homage to the most memorable&nbsp;
fight scene from Daredevil's Netflix series.

00:01:38.880 --> 00:01:44.280
While "She-Hulk" Episode 8 doesn't address&nbsp;
how much of the Netflix "Daredevil" series&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.280 --> 00:01:49.980
remains canonical within the MCU, it does pay&nbsp;
homage to that show's signature hallway fight&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.980 --> 00:01:54.120
scenes. Though not nearly as brutal&nbsp;
as the original scene it references,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.120 --> 00:01:57.600
it does make sense that an MCU show would stray&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.600 --> 00:02:03.300
from that level of darkness and brutality.&nbsp;
And besides, he's got She-Hulk as a backup.

00:02:03.300 --> 00:02:07.380
Jen and Matt are forced to team up in&nbsp;
order to stop a minor villain, Leapfrog,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.380 --> 00:02:13.560
who kidnaps their tailor, Luke Jacobson. They&nbsp;
track Leapfrog to his not-so-secret lair,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.560 --> 00:02:20.160
where he has the seamster hostage. Jen wants to&nbsp;
bust inside She-Hulk style, but Matt insists on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.160 --> 00:02:25.860
stealth. He enters the building to find a&nbsp;
hallway full of goons… or maybe henchmen?

00:02:25.860 --> 00:02:28.320
"Goons and henchmen are two&nbsp;
completely different animals."

00:02:28.320 --> 00:02:33.180
Daredevil easily picks off the first few&nbsp;
men, but then backup arrives. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.180 --> 00:02:36.780
before they can attack, She-Hulk&nbsp;
busts in through the ceiling,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.780 --> 00:02:40.620
crushing them. Speaking to Marvel,&nbsp;
"She-Hulk" writer Jessica Gao said,

00:02:40.620 --> 00:02:44.340
"It was just natural for this show, being so meta,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.340 --> 00:02:49.020
that we would acknowledge and tease the&nbsp;
classic Daredevil hallway fight. But then,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.020 --> 00:02:52.740
of course, we would have to undercut&nbsp;
it with our girl, She-Hulk."

00:02:52.740 --> 00:02:57.120
The original hallway fight "She-Hulk"&nbsp;
is referencing was a crucial aspect of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:57.120 --> 00:03:01.560
the "Daredevil" Netflix series. As the first&nbsp;
of the "Defenders" shows to land on Netflix,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.560 --> 00:03:06.480
no one knew quite what to expect when its&nbsp;
first season dropped in 2015. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.480 --> 00:03:09.960
the series quickly won the&nbsp;
praise of critics and fans,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.960 --> 00:03:16.200
boasting a 99% critic score on Rotten&nbsp;
Tomatoes, One review from IndieWire noted,

00:03:16.200 --> 00:03:19.560
"All of [the fight scenes] stack up as some of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.560 --> 00:03:22.980
best-realized fight scenes I've&nbsp;
seen in a television context."

00:03:22.980 --> 00:03:27.780
The pièce de résistance among the fight&nbsp;
scenes on "Daredevil" comes with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:27.780 --> 00:03:33.180
hallway battle in Episode 2, and it's a&nbsp;
visceral thrill. With his super hearing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.180 --> 00:03:37.680
Daredevil hears a kidnapped child locked&nbsp;
in a basement and decides to rescue him.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:37.680 --> 00:03:42.780
We don't actually see Matt take out the first&nbsp;
roomful of goons, as the camera pans across to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:42.780 --> 00:03:48.840
an adjacent hallway. That door breaks open and&nbsp;
Daredevil stumbles through, swarmed by thugs.

00:03:48.840 --> 00:03:52.320
The entire action scene is&nbsp;
shot in one, continuous take,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.320 --> 00:03:57.300
the camera's refusal to cut away heightening&nbsp;
the brutality of the fight. By the time Matt&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:57.300 --> 00:04:02.160
takes out the last of the child traffickers,&nbsp;
he can barely pick himself off the floor.

00:04:02.160 --> 00:04:06.600
Unlike the hallway fights on Netflix's&nbsp;
"Daredevil," Matt suffers no injuries&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.600 --> 00:04:11.880
during the "She-Hulk'' fight. Maybe that means&nbsp;
that ol' Hornhead has gotten a bit better at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.880 --> 00:04:17.760
pacing himself for long fights, or maybe the MCU&nbsp;
version of Daredevil is just that much better.

